---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE_TITLE } from "../../consts";

const repo = "https://github.com/leric/cstack";
const installCommand = "npx skills add leric/cstack";
const description =
	"Five coding agent skills for exploring domains, specifying product behavior, designing changes, learning from implementation, and keeping project knowledge current.";

const stages = [
	{
		skill: "cs-explore",
		title: "Explore the domain",
		example:
			"Look at how assessors select standards today. Use real equipment examples to clarify which attributes determine applicability and where expert judgment is needed.",
	},
	{
		skill: "cs-spec",
		title: "Specify product behavior",
		example:
			"Define when to suggest standards, who confirms them, and when the app creates assessment tasks. If the confirmation flow is hard to picture, sketch it in a standalone HTML file. Record agreed behavior, acceptance scenarios, and scope in the change file.",
	},
	{
		skill: "cs-design",
		title: "Design the code change",
		example:
			"Find where applicability rules belong, which interfaces and tests must change together, and which decisions need a human answer before the coding agent implements the plan.",
	},
	{
		skill: "cs-reflect",
		title: "Reflect while the implementation is fresh",
		example:
			"Review the live implementation trace for hard-to-find context, bypassed boundaries, and unclear ownership. Discuss any undocumented exception with the owner, make approved refactors, and record what the next session needs in the change file.",
	},
	{
		skill: "cs-merge",
		title: "Update Living Knowledge when the change lands",
		example:
			"At merge time, even in a fresh session, check the change file and reflection notes against the final git diff. If the app now waits for assessor confirmation, propose updating the relevant guidance and retiring the old rule. After approval, apply the edits, record any deferred refactors for follow-up, and retire the completed change file.",
	},
];
---

<BaseLayout title={`cstack — ${SITE_TITLE}`} description={description}>
	<div class="cstack-page">
		<header class="hero">
			<h1>cstack</h1>
			<p class="tagline">For code you’ll have to maintain</p>
			<p class="lead">{description}</p>
			<p>
				The name cstack comes from <a href="/cmp/">Context-Cost-Driven Software Design</a>,
				which evaluates a design by how much effort it takes to gather the context needed
				to make a correct change.
			</p>

			<div class="install">
				<div class="command">
					<code id="install-command">{installCommand}</code>
					<button id="copy-install" type="button" hidden aria-label="Copy install command">Copy</button>
				</div>
				<a href={repo}>View on GitHub</a>
			</div>
			<p id="copy-status" class="copy-status" role="status"></p>
		</header>

		<section aria-labelledby="problems-heading">
			<h2 id="problems-heading">The hard problems cstack tackles</h2>
			<dl class="problems">
				<div>
					<dt>Who decides what the software should do?</dt>
					<dd>
						A feature request can hide business rules. If a standard is only recommended,
						should the app create tasks immediately or wait for an assessor to confirm it?
						cstack uses Domain Ontology to clarify the rules, surfaces open decisions for
						human judgment, and records the agreed behavior in the change spec.
					</dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>How much of the system do you need to understand to make one change?</dt>
					<dd>
						A small request can touch several modules. If the same rule lives in several
						places, the next change can miss one. cstack applies the
						<a href="/cmp/">Context Minimization Principle</a> to decide where each rule
						belongs and identify what must change together.
					</dd>
				</div>
				<div>
					<dt>How do you keep project knowledge current as the code changes?</dt>
					<dd>
						The final diff can show how the agent handled an undocumented exception while
						leaving its reason unclear. With
						<a href="https://www.airic.dev/research/reflective-bootstrapping/">Reflective Bootstrapping</a>,
						<code>/cs-reflect</code> captures that reasoning while the implementation trace
						is still available. At merge time, <code>/cs-merge</code> checks it against the
						final diff and proposes edits only to the relevant parts of the project’s Living
						Knowledge. It can add a rule, resolve conflicting guidance, consolidate scattered
						exceptions, or remove guidance the code or tests now enforce.
					</dd>
				</div>
			</dl>
		</section>

		<section aria-labelledby="workflow-heading">
			<h2 id="workflow-heading">The cstack workflow in practice</h2>
			<p>
				Suppose a customer asks the app to recommend compliance standards when a user selects
				an equipment type, then generate assessment tasks.
			</p>
			<ol class="workflow">
				{stages.map(({ skill, title, example }) => (
					<li>
						<div class="stage-heading">
							<h3>{title}</h3>
							<a href={`${repo}/tree/main/skills/${skill}`}><code>/{skill}</code></a>
						</div>
						<p>{example}</p>
					</li>
				))}
			</ol>
			<p>
				For a clear bug fix or refactor, start with <code>/cs-design</code>, implement the
				change, then run <code>/cs-reflect</code>. Use <code>/cs-merge</code> if reflection
				leaves knowledge updates or deferred refactors to settle. If a design question
				turns out to be a product decision, go back to <code>/cs-spec</code>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<p class="project-links">
			<a href={`${repo}#readme`}>Read the full usage guide</a>
			<a href="/skills/legacy/">Original CMP Skills archive</a>
		</p>
	</div>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
	const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>("#copy-install");
	const command = document.getElementById("install-command");
	const status = document.getElementById("copy-status");

	if (button && command && status && navigator.clipboard) {
		button.hidden = false;
		button.addEventListener("click", async () => {
			try {
				await navigator.clipboard.writeText(command.textContent ?? "");
				status.textContent = "Install command copied.";
			} catch {
				status.textContent = "Select and copy the command manually.";
			}
		});
	}
</script>

<style>
	.cstack-page {
		width: min(calc(100vw - 2 * var(--content-padding)), var(--reading-width));
		padding: clamp(32px, 6vw, 64px) 0 24px;
	}

	.hero {
		border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
		padding-bottom: 16px;
	}

	.hero h1 {
		font-size: clamp(42px, 7vw, 64px);
		letter-spacing: -0.045em;
	}

	.tagline {
		font-size: clamp(21px, 4vw, 30px);
		line-height: 1.35;
		font-weight: 600;
		text-wrap: balance;
	}

	.install,
	.command,
	.project-links,
	.stage-heading {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 8px 16px;
	}

	.command {
		max-width: 100%;
		padding: 6px 8px 6px 12px;
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		border-radius: var(--card-radius);
		background: var(--background-alt);
	}

	.command code {
		background: transparent;
		padding: 0;
		border: 0;
		overflow-wrap: anywhere;
	}

	.command button {
		font: inherit;
		font-size: 0.85rem;
		color: var(--accent);
		background: var(--background);
		border: 1px solid var(--border);
		border-radius: 4px;
		padding: 4px 9px;
		cursor: pointer;
	}

	.copy-status {
		min-height: 1.7em;
		margin: 4px 0 0;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: 0.85rem;
	}

	.workflow {
		padding-left: 1.65rem;
		margin: 0 0 20px;
	}

	.workflow li {
		padding: 14px 0 14px 4px;
		margin: 0;
		border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
	}

	.workflow li::marker {
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
	}

	.workflow h3 {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 1.125rem;
	}

	.workflow p {
		margin: 5px 0 0;
	}

	.problems {
		display: grid;
		gap: 14px;
		margin: 0 0 28px;
	}

	.problems dt {
		font-weight: 600;
	}

	.problems dd {
		margin: 3px 0 0;
		color: var(--secondary);
	}

	.project-links {
		border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
		padding-top: 18px;
	}
</style>
